{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":780249,"duration_api_ms":839190,"ttft_ms":3329,"ttft_stream_ms":2398,"time_to_request_ms":186,"num_turns":73,"result":"This all looks clean and correct. The implementation is complete. Summary of what was done:\n\n**Core mechanism** (`lib/promscrape/`):\n- New global flag `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` (default `0` = unlimited) plus a per-job `max_scrape_targets` YAML override in `scrape_configs` (`config.go`), following the exact same global-default/per-job-override pattern as `series_limit`.\n- The effective limit is baked into every `ScrapeWork` for a job (`scrapework.go`), so it's uniform across whichever discovery mechanism produced it.\n- Enforcement point: `targetStatusMap.RegisterIfBelowLimit` (`targetstatus.go`) — since `tsmGlobal` is the one structure that already aggregates active-target counts per job across *all* discovery mechanisms (each of which runs as its own independent `scraperGroup`), it's the natural cross-cutting choke point. `scraper.go`'s scraper-start loop now calls it before spinning up a new scraper; rejected targets are registered into the existing `droppedTargetsMap` with a new `targetDropReasonTargetsLimit` reason instead of being started.\n- Because dropped targets reuse the existing `droppedTargetsMap`, they automatically show up wherever dropped targets already show up: `/service-discovery` (per-job active/dropped counts + reason badge) and `/api/v1/targets`'s `droppedTargets` — no UI/template changes needed. This also applies identically to the single-node binary, since it shares this library.\n\n**Tests** added covering all three required cases:\n- `config_test.go`: global default flows through, per-job override wins, explicit `0` disables it.\n- `targetstatus_test.go`: unit test of `RegisterIfBelowLimit` for under-limit, over-limit (truncation), and unlimited(default).\n- `scraper_test.go`: end-to-end test spinning up a real `scraper